&lt;p&gt;I’ll gladly take this (and any) opportunity to share the following wonderful meme a user named AsherWare posted in 2016 on &lt;a href=&quot;https://imgur.com/gallery/brush-up-on-ornithology-60nAHCJ&quot;&gt;imgur&lt;/a&gt; (its original source is unknown, this is the earliest known appearance of its usage):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;
  &lt;img src=&quot;/media/british-tits.jpeg&quot; alt=&quot;British tits.&quot; /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
  The colossal tit (&lt;em&gt;parus colossus&lt;/em&gt;, definitely not Linnaeus, somewhere 2016-ish) is one of the most destructive of the tit species on the British Isles, known for its crushing of hopes and dreams through its frequent media appearances and conservative policies. Its largest single-event impact thus far is the shrinkage of the UK’s GDP by at least 6%.¹
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;¹ &lt;a href=&quot;https://siepr.stanford.edu/publications/working-paper/economic-impact-brexit&quot;&gt;Bloom &lt;em&gt;et al.&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;The Economic Impact of Brexit&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</content><author><name></name></author><summary type="html">There’s a feeling I get, when I look to the West, except when crane migration is underway, which is when I am usually more fond of the East. For now, no significant eastern wind has blown any cranes off-track, so I was particularly excited to head eastward to Köln. Unfortunately, I learned that Köln lies beyond the typical western migration route. Indeed, barely any cranes are reported over Cologne, although I am wary of the data, which comes from a user base heavily concentrated in Belgium and The Netherlands (see below, Source: observation.org and OpenTopoMap).</summary></entry><entry><title type="html">East Canada unknowns</title><link href="http://localhost:4000/2026/10/23/evening.html" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="East Canada unknowns" /><published>2026-10-23T00:00:00+02:00</published><updated>2026-10-23T00:00:00+02:00</updated><id>http://localhost:4000/2026/10/23/evening</id><content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost:4000/2026/10/23/evening.html">&lt;p&gt;Since the August east Canada trip of 2026, there have been lurking in my mind a couple of unidentified birds. For some, I have an idea but no ID. In such cases, the bird enthusiasts over at r/whatsthisbird are always keen to help. I asked for IDs of the following east Canadian birds ranging from Toronto to Tadoussac.&lt;/p&gt;

