&lt;p&gt;Strong consensus on r/whatsthisbird that my hunch of a female &lt;a href=&quot;https://ebird.org/species/bnhcow&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot;&gt;brown-headed cowbird&lt;/a&gt; for UB1 (unknown bird #1) was correct: the streaking on the body does not contrast enough for a female red-winged blackbird. UB2 is a sparrow, but it could be &lt;a href=&quot;https://ebird.org/species/linspa&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot;&gt;Lincoln’s sparrow&lt;/a&gt; or a swamp or song sparrow. Strong narrow streaking on the belly points to Lincoln’s sparrow, but there’s no reports of those in early August. A swamp sparrow is more likely. UB2 will remain unsolved without hearing the bird sing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Likewise, UB3 remains a mystery. Flycatchers are a nightmare to distinguish if you don’t hear them sing, especially if you only have a picture of their belly side. Even Peter, former Algonquin park guide that we met on the trail who was also looking at this very same bird, couldn’t tell which bird it was. Maybe a future post should detail our lovely encounter with Peter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;UB4 has two plausible options: a breeding female &lt;a href=&quot;https://ebird.org/species/bkpwar&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot;&gt;Blackpoll warbler&lt;/a&gt; or immature female &lt;a href=&quot;https://ebird.org/species/pinwar&quot;&gt;pine warbler&lt;/a&gt; could work, but the overall lack of colour in the picture leads me to have reservations about definitively saying this is X or Y.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thrushes give me ID headaches, so for UB5 we are lucky that reddit thinks this is a Swainson’s thrush (&lt;em&gt;dwerglijster&lt;/em&gt;). There’s a faint pale eye ring, the spotting on the body seems to match, and I remember that my overall impression of this bird was that it was smaller than a blackbird. With some confidence we can therefore ID UB5 as &lt;a href=&quot;https://ebird.org/species/swathr&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot;&gt;Swainson’s thrush&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;UB6 can be a ‘generic’ &lt;a href=&quot;https://ebird.org/species/bkhgul&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot;&gt;black-headed gull&lt;/a&gt; (&lt;em&gt;kokmeeuw&lt;/em&gt;), a &lt;a href=&quot;https://ebird.org/species/fragul&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot;&gt;Franklin’s gull&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href=&quot;https://ebird.org/species/bongul&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot;&gt;Bonaparte’s gull&lt;/a&gt; (&lt;em&gt;kleine kokmeeuw&lt;/em&gt;). The picture does not allow to see whether there is a thick white eye ring or what the colour of the legs is (this picture was taken with my phone as we were on the whale watching boat at the time and I didn’t dare take my camera out for risk of whale splashing), so we turn to the ranges of these gulls on allaboutbirds.org. On August 12 there’s only one of those three &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/browse/filter/loc/ChIJy06QX8DVv0wRWZXLMjSDQxM/date/8-12/behavior/all/size/all/colors/all/sort/family/view/list-view&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot;&gt;which occurs very regularly in Tadoussac&lt;/a&gt;. Additionally, I feel like my camera should be able to record the much wider white eye ring on Franklin’s. UB6 is therefore a Bonaparte’s gull, as also suggested by reddit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://ebird.org/species/leafly&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot;&gt;Least flycatcher&lt;/a&gt; seems to work best for UB7, given the relatively bold pale eye ring (other flycatchers such as Adler’s have thinner eye rings), the two pale wing bars and the light-coloured underside (others are more olive/buff). So least flycatcher for UB7 it is!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All in all, pulling 4 new bird species out of these pictures without accompanying sound bits is more than I had dared to hope for, bringing the species total to 151.&lt;/p&gt;</content><author><name></name></author><summary type="html">Since the August east Canada trip of 2026, there have been lurking in my mind a couple of unidentified birds. For some, I have an idea but no ID. In such cases, the bird enthusiasts over at r/whatsthisbird are always keen to help. I asked for IDs of the following east Canadian birds ranging from Toronto to Tadoussac.</summary></entry><entry><title type="html">Crane hunt part 1</title><link href="http://localhost:4000/2026/10/21/morning.html" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Crane hunt part 1" /><published>2026-10-21T00:00:00+02:00</published><updated>2026-10-21T00:00:00+02:00</updated><id>http://localhost:4000/2026/10/21/morning</id><content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost:4000/2026/10/21/morning.html">&lt;p&gt;Common crane (&lt;em&gt;grus grus&lt;/em&gt;) migration through Europe is underway from Scandinavia and northern Germany to the south. There’s scattered reports all over the eastern parts of Belgium, exploding in the last couple of days. Chances are slim that a lot of the action happens over Ghent, which would require eastern winds blowing birds west. With dominant west and south-west wind this week, it’s highly unlikely that any cranes will be spotted over Bourgoyen. But I made fresh coffee and I have a warm jacket, so out we go.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No cranes yet. Luckily, there’s always the cute long-tailed tit to fall back on. A lot of barnacle geese flying over and resting at Afgraving. If the map on Wikipedia is to be trusted, these are mostly the Siberian population of barnacle geese, which winters in The Netherlands, Germany and Belgium. Fun wiki-fact: the Dutch common name and scientific name (&lt;em&gt;brandgans&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;branta leucopsis&lt;/em&gt;) apparently derive from Old Norse &lt;em&gt;Brandgás&lt;/em&gt;, meaning ‘burnt goose’.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;/media/long-tailed-tit2.jpeg&quot; alt=&quot;One long-tailed tit.&quot; /&gt;
&lt;em&gt;One long-tailed tit near Afgraving on 20 October 2026.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;/media/barnacle-geese.jpeg&quot; alt=&quot;Barnacle geese.&quot; /&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Barnacle geese flying south over Afgraving on 20 October 2026.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I’m free on Saturday, so I’m thinking of a plan to perhaps try to head east to catch some migrating cranes. I’m also dying to find a nutcracker, multiple of which have poured down south into Belgium, one seemingly residing for a couple of days now in &lt;a href=&quot;https://waarnemingen.be/locations/28822/&quot;&gt;Heverleebos&lt;/a&gt;. Hard to spot cranes in a dense forest, though.&lt;/p&gt;</content><author><name></name></author><summary type="html">Common crane (grus grus) migration through Europe is underway from Scandinavia and northern Germany to the south. There’s scattered reports all over the eastern parts of Belgium, exploding in the last couple of days. Chances are slim that a lot of the action happens over Ghent, which would require eastern winds blowing birds west. With dominant west and south-west wind this week, it’s highly unlikely that any cranes will be spotted over Bourgoyen. But I made fresh coffee and I have a warm jacket, so out we go.</summary></entry><entry><title type="html">Early birding</title><link href="http://localhost:4000/2026/10/19/morning.html" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Early birding" /><published>2026-10-19T00:00:00+02:00</published><updated>2026-10-19T00:00:00+02:00</updated><id>http://localhost:4000/2026/10/19/morning</id><content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost:4000/2026/10/19/morning.html">&lt;p&gt;The early bird has to drive to Antwerp on a Sunday morning, but the even earlier birder manages to get some birding done even before that. No groundbreaking observations but a very photogenic wren and a kestrel being harassed by two crows. The shot of the kestrel illustrates that other inconvenient truth a friendly birder holding a 600 mm lens told me recently: you can never have enough millimeters.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;/media/winterkoning.jpeg&quot; alt=&quot;Eurasian wren posing on a Sunday morning in Bourgoyen.&quot; /&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Eurasian wren posing on a Sunday morning in Bourgoyen on 18 October 2026.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div style=&quot;text-align: left; clear: both;&quot;&gt;
  &lt;img src=&quot;/media/kestrel-spread.jpeg&quot; alt=&quot;Centered Image&quot; style=&quot;max-width: 800px; height: auto;&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Common kestrel landing in a field near Valkenhuis.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Monday mornings are extremely quiet once you get out in the fields compared to Sundays. Without human distractions, I manage to spot a female hen harrier flying north over the Oude Kale valley. Unlike what the Dutch name would have you believe (blauwe kiekendief), the female harrier is brown and not blue, with a distinctive white rump.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;/media/blauwe-kiekendief.jpeg&quot; alt=&quot;Female hen harrier flying over Merendree on 20 October 2026.&quot; /&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Female hen harrier flying over Merendree on 20 October 2026.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Along the track I hear (multiple?) lesser spotted woodpeckers calling. I can’t find them, so the picture provided with the audio bite is by Yves Hoebeke from waarnemingen.be. Unlike the far more common greater spotted woodpecker, the lesser does not have a red lower belly.&lt;/p&gt;

